WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:04.290
So our reaction’s ready. Now all we need
again is a little bit of water just to start

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:08.880
it off.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.440
So we’re here today and we’ve come back
after hours because we need to use this room

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.680
for a long
period of time because we’re going to do

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:18.109
something quite exciting. Because Brady’s
bought another

00:00:18.109 --> 00:00:21.759
camera which you can see he’s setup over
there and we’re going to use that second

00:00:21.759 --> 00:00:25.970
camera to capture
one second of film every minute for maybe

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:28.710
two or three hours and we’re going to do
some time lapse.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:33.300
Something I’ve seen on TV with like flowers
opening or plants growing but never with chemistry

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.300
developing.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:42.600
Silver is one of the few metallic elements
that occurs naturally as the metal in nature.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:48.680
So that if you’re
lucky you can wander around and find lumps

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:52.870
of silver lying around on the ground. And
this is how, in

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:58.660
ancient times, silver was discovered so it
has been known for thousands if not tens of

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:04.190
thousands of
years. Gold is somewhat similar and also copper.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:07.750
But most other metals combine too easily with
oxygen

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:11.770
or other elements so you don’t find them
naturally.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:16.650
So I thought what we’d do today is through
a competition reaction and that competition

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.151
reaction is based
on something called the reactivity series

00:01:19.151 --> 00:01:22.720
of metals. So we’re going to compare the
reactivity of two

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:26.700
metals. It’s a bit like a game of football.
We’re going to give one of those metals

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.950
the football and in this
case it’s another component or another salt

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:34.830
component, nitrate. So the two metals that
are going to be

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:40.890
competing for the nitrate are silver and copper.
And really these are related by something

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.060
called the
reactivity series. One of these metals is

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:47.340
going to win, I know that and I hope we’ll
find it during this

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:48.340
video.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:54.720
Silver had a particular use in photography
and in the old days when people used photographic

00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:01.420
film the
black colour that you saw on developed negatives

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:08.560
is in fact silver. A photographic film consists
of a layer

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:16.190
of silver bromide or sometimes silver iodide
and when the light shines on it the light

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:22.040
just starts a tiny
amount of reaction making silver and then

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:27.420
when you put it in the developing solution
the catalytic

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:32.670
amount of silver that you’ve produced causes
a big reaction and you make a large amount

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:37.890
of silver. And
so you get this black colour which then you

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:39.090
can print as pictures.

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:44.569
So on the bench I’ve got a small beaker
or an Erlenmeyer flask, as we chemists call

00:02:44.569 --> 00:02:49.319
it, which is full of
deionised water. So we’ve passed the water

00:02:49.319 --> 00:02:52.180
through a machine which has taken out all
of the other ions

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:55.880
so that they can’t play around with the
competition reaction that we want to start

00:02:55.880 --> 00:03:00.810
today. Firstly, I’m going
to start off by giving the prize of the competition

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:05.410
to silver. So here I’ve got a sample of
silver nitrate. It’s

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:09.240
sensitive to light so I can’t leave it out
on the bench very often. So now what I want

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.710
to do, I’m going to
put some into the water to make a silver nitrate

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:15.040
solution. So I want to make sure that there’s
plenty in

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:23.480
there so that it’s saturated. Now we’ve
got to put in the competitor. So what we’re

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:25.980
going to do, we’re
going to take some copper, copper metal. And

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.680
this is just a sample of wire which I’ve
rubbed with some

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:33.550
wire wool just to make it sort of nice and
shiny. And now all I’m going to do is dangle

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:36.190
it into the silver
nitrate. Then it’s going to start to compete

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:38.940
for the nitrate and we’ll see what happens.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:44.390
So Brady’s started the recording and I think
the best thing for us to do is to go away

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:47.490
and do something a
little bit more exciting and come back and

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:48.990
have a look in an hour.

00:03:48.990 --> 00:04:00.290
Silver has become quite popular recently as
a potential new way of making things anti-bacterial:

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:01.940
killing
bacteria.

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:05.920
So now we’re going to make some flash powder
and the flash powder we’re going to make

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:09.140
today is a
two component mixture. It’s very finely

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:15.830
ground magnesium and the second component
is ground silver

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:21.669
nitrate, solid OK. So we take these two components
and we mix the two together very intimately

00:04:21.669 --> 00:04:23.580
and
Neil’s just weighing the materials out right

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:28.449
now. So we’re going to put the magnesium
and the silver

00:04:28.449 --> 00:04:34.211
nitrate, the two powders, together into a
small plastic container here. We’re going

00:04:34.211 --> 00:04:36.979
to mix them very,
very carefully and then we’re going to see

00:04:36.979 --> 00:04:40.870
what happens when we initiate a chemical reaction.

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:46.979
For hundreds of years people have used silver
spoons. Partly, I think, because silver is

00:04:46.979 --> 00:04:51.830
quite easy to
make but also because the silver does have

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:58.219
properties that kill bacteria so a silver
spoon is less likely to

00:04:58.219 --> 00:05:03.589
get harmful bacteria on it than if you have
one that’s made out of wood or horn of a

00:05:03.589 --> 00:05:06.949
cow or something
like that.

00:05:06.949 --> 00:05:12.330
So we’re mixing these very carefully, in
the fume hood, just in case the chemistry

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:15.029
starts before we want
it to.

00:05:15.029 --> 00:05:21.349
And in recent times people have started making
very, very fine particles of silver, so called

00:05:21.349 --> 00:05:30.180
nanoparticles, which can exist in solutions.
So here I’ve got a solution of silver nanoparticles.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:32.249
You can
see it doesn’t look silver it looks a bit

00:05:32.249 --> 00:05:39.039
yellow actually and you can tell that it’s
got particles in it because,

00:05:39.039 --> 00:05:45.999
if I can find a torch… So here we’ve got
particles and you can see the particles better

00:05:45.999 --> 00:05:51.180
if we shine a light
through it and if you look at the light you

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:55.939
can see that there is a cone of light that
looks rather like the

00:05:55.939 --> 00:06:03.110
headlights of a car in fog. And this is an
effect that’s called the Tyndall Cone which

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:07.099
indicates that there
are very, very tiny particles suspended in

00:06:07.099 --> 00:06:12.909
the solution. So this is not silver dissolved
in the water but

00:06:12.909 --> 00:06:15.569
actually very tiny particles.

00:06:15.569 --> 00:06:25.939
So let’s start the reaction, just with a
little bit of water. Wow! I think we should

00:06:25.939 --> 00:06:27.490
do it again though.

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:29.629
Yeah!
LAUGHTER

00:06:29.629 --> 00:06:34.830
People are now using the silver nanoparticles
for all sorts of purposes. Here you can see

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:38.979
that they’ve
been put onto some sort of plastic and you

00:06:38.979 --> 00:06:43.240
can now buy socks which have silver nanoparticles
on them

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:47.789
which are claimed to kill the bacteria that
make your feet smell.

00:06:47.789 --> 00:06:51.220
So we’re going to repeat the reaction. B-B-B-B…
SQUAWCKS

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:55.339
That’s going to come out somewhere isn’t
it?

00:06:55.339 --> 00:07:00.589
Silver will conduct electricity, it will also
conduct heat. If you’ve ever tried stirring

00:07:00.589 --> 00:07:06.119
tea with a silver spoon
it gets very hot, you tend to drop it. It

00:07:06.119 --> 00:07:08.469
also conducts electricity well.

00:07:08.469 --> 00:07:12.099
So we’re going to repeat the reaction so
that you can see it again, because that was

00:07:12.099 --> 00:07:14.970
really fast. This
time we’re going to do it on a glass dish

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:22.559
so you can see it a bit better. We’ll pour
out a small amount.

00:07:22.559 --> 00:07:24.719
LAUGHTER

00:07:24.719 --> 00:07:28.210
So our reaction’s ready. Now all we need
again is a little bit of water just to start

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:29.210
it off.

00:07:29.210 --> 00:07:33.059
Did you catch that?

00:07:33.059 --> 00:07:37.210
I managed to close my eyes this time(!)

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:46.129
During the Second World War, when the US government
was involved in enriching uranium as part

00:07:46.129 --> 00:07:49.749
of
the Manhattan Project they needed an enormous

00:07:49.749 --> 00:08:00.149
amount of wire to make magnets. And so a huge
amount of silver was taken from the US treasury

00:08:00.149 --> 00:08:04.849
where it was stored as silver to give value
to the US

00:08:04.849 --> 00:08:12.139
dollar and was turned into wires to make magnets.
And I believe that the silver was never returned

00:08:12.139 --> 00:08:14.410
or at
least it wasn’t returned at the end of the

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:16.389
war as was promised.

00:08:16.389 --> 00:08:21.789
Well, I’ve managed to fill maybe one minute’s
worth of time by now maybe two because we’ve

00:08:21.789 --> 00:08:23.009
been
away about two hours. So we’re going to

00:08:23.009 --> 00:08:27.120
go back and have a look at the flask to see
if there are any

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:33.090
changes. If you remember when we left it was
a nice clear solution of silver nitrate with

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:36.770
some copper
wire which was just immersed inside. So let’s

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:38.709
go and have a look.

00:08:38.709 --> 00:08:45.240
There’s been big changes in our flask! So
if you look carefully you can see now that

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:51.600
the copper wire
itself is covered in very, very sharp crystals

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:56.310
of silver which have basically become deposited
on the

00:08:56.310 --> 00:09:02.020
surface. Copper has now won this chemical
reaction. It’s now going into the solution;

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:07.240
it’s taking the salt
element and precipitating solid silver crystals.

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:10.459
So if you look carefully, you can see that
the colour of the

00:09:10.459 --> 00:09:16.270
solution has changed. It was colourless, now
it’s blue which tells me there is another

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:18.290
ion, copper, in the
solution.

